ITEMS = ((('what is the recovery position', 'how do i put someone in the recovery position', 'when should i use the recovery position', 'how to place a person in recovery position', 'what is the recovery position used for', 'how do you roll someone into recovery position', 'what does recovery position mean in first aid', 'how to position an unconscious person in recovery', 'why is the recovery position important', 'who should learn the recovery position'), 'The recovery position is a safe side-lying position for someone who is breathing but not fully conscious. It helps keep the airway clear and reduces the risk of choking on vomit. Call emergency services first, and get hands-on practice on a first-aid course.'), (('how to roll a person into the recovery position', 'what are the steps for the recovery position', 'which arm goes where in recovery position', 'how to support the head in recovery position', 'should i kneel beside someone for recovery position', 'how to move legs into the recovery position', 'what angle is best for recovery position', 'can i roll a large person into recovery position', 'how to turn someone gently into recovery position', 'what if the recovery position feels awkward'), 'Kneel beside the person, place the nearest arm at right angles and bring the far arm across the chest. Roll them towards you, supporting the head, and bend the upper leg to steady the body. Ask emergency services for guidance if moving them is difficult.'), (('should i put a breathing person in the recovery position', 'when is the recovery position appropriate', 'what if the person is awake but drowsy in the recovery position', 'should i use recovery position after fainting', 'is the recovery position right after a fit', 'situations where the recovery position is not suitable', 'what if the person is breathing normally in the recovery position', 'how to decide about the recovery position', 'should i use recovery position after choking', 'when to keep someone on their back instead in the recovery position'), 'Use the recovery position when someone is breathing normally but has a reduced level of consciousness. If they are awake and fully alert, let them rest in a comfortable position. Do not move them if a spinal injury is suspected unless their airway is at risk.'), (('how long should someone stay in the recovery position', 'how often to check breathing in recovery position', 'can i leave someone in recovery position alone', 'what if breathing changes in recovery position', 'should i keep watching a person in recovery position', 'how to monitor someone in the recovery position', 'what if the person vomits in recovery position', 'when to move someone out of recovery position', 'how long until help arrives in recovery position', 'what to do while waiting in recovery position'), 'Stay with the person and check their breathing regularly while they are in the recovery position. Keep their airway open and be ready to start CPR if breathing stops. Call emergency services and remain nearby until a qualified person arrives.'), (('should i use the recovery position after cpr', 'how to place someone in recovery after compressions', 'what if breathing returns during cpr in the recovery position', 'can i use recovery position after a defibrillator', 'how to move a casualty after rescue breaths in the recovery position', 'when to roll after giving cpr in the recovery position', 'what to do once a person starts breathing again in the recovery position', 'should i stop compressions for recovery position', 'how to hand over after cpr and recovery', 'who decides on recovery position after resuscitation'), 'If a person starts breathing normally during CPR, stop compressions and roll them into the recovery position. Keep checking breathing and stay with them. Tell the ambulance crew what happened and when breathing returned.'), (('can i put a child in the recovery position', 'how to place a baby in recovery position', 'is the recovery position safe for infants', 'how to adapt recovery position for children', 'should i use recovery position for a toddler', 'what to check when rolling a child in the recovery position', 'how to keep a child warm in recovery position', 'when to call help for a child in recovery position', 'what if a child is very small in the recovery position', 'who should check a child after recovery position'), 'A child who is breathing but not fully conscious can be placed on their side with the head turned to let fluid drain. Use a smaller roll and keep them warm. Call emergency services and get a doctor to check the child, especially if they are very young.'), (('is the recovery position safe after a head injury', 'should i move someone with a suspected spinal injury in the recovery position', 'what if i cannot roll a person without pain in the recovery position', 'can recovery position make an injury worse', 'how to protect the neck in recovery position', 'what if the person has a leg injury in the recovery position', 'when to avoid moving someone into recovery', 'how to balance airway and spinal safety in the recovery position', 'what to do if moving causes pain in the recovery position', 'who can advise about moving an injured person in the recovery position'), 'If a spinal or neck injury is possible, avoid moving the person unless their airway is at risk, and use the recovery position only if necessary to keep them safe. Call emergency services for advice. Keep their head and neck as still as you can while waiting.'), (('how do i keep the airway open in recovery position', 'what if the tongue blocks the airway in the recovery position', 'should i tilt the head in recovery position', 'how to check the mouth in recovery position', 'what if fluid comes from the mouth in the recovery position', 'how to clear vomit in recovery position', 'should i put anything under the head in the recovery position', 'what to do if breathing sounds noisy in the recovery position', 'how to watch the chest in recovery position', 'when to call for help with the airway in the recovery position'), 'Once on their side, tilt the head slightly back and turn the face towards the ground so fluids can drain. Do not put a pillow under the head. Call emergency services if breathing becomes noisy or stops, and be ready to begin CPR.'), (('how do i keep someone warm in recovery position', 'should i cover a person in recovery position', 'what if the ground is cold in the recovery position', 'can i give a drink in recovery position', 'should i give food to someone in recovery position', 'how to shield a person from the weather in the recovery position', 'what if the person is shivering in the recovery position', 'how to comfort someone in recovery position', 'should i check for other injuries in the recovery position', 'when to ask an ambulance crew about warming in the recovery position'), 'Cover the person with a coat or blanket while they are in the recovery position and shield them from wind or rain. Do not give food or drink while consciousness is reduced. Call emergency services and watch for shivering or other injuries.'), (('what training covers the recovery position', 'should i practise the recovery position', 'what does a first aid course teach about recovery position', 'how to practise recovery position safely', 'can i learn recovery position online', 'who can show me the recovery position', 'is it hard to hold someone in recovery position', 'what if i cannot lift a person alone in the recovery position', 'how to ask bystanders to help with recovery', 'when to get help positioning a casualty'), 'The best way to learn the recovery position is to practise on a recognised first-aid course with an instructor. If you need to move someone and cannot manage alone, ask a bystander to help and call emergency services. Never practise on a real person who is unwell.'))
